 6. Labels the root pane `posse:<project>:t<seq>`, writes `brief.md` and `launch.md`, then starts the agent with `agent.start` (name `posse-<project>-t<seq>-<launch>`, cut to 32 chars and never derived from Identity, where `<launch>` counts launches of this Task so a relaunch never collides with a name Herdr has not released yet; args = kind `auto_approve_args` + `model_args`/`effort_args` filled from the Profile + Profile `args`, preceded for Claude by the attribution-off `--settings` unless the Profile sets `attribution = true`). The canonical pane label and all internal identifiers remain unchanged. `launches` and its Launch Identity Record (Task id, launch number, Profile name, configured model value and whether that value is determinable) are committed together before each `agent.start`; relaunches append records and never replace earlier identities. Herdr types each launch and each exported variable as one shell line. A line over the 4095-byte limit of a canonical-mode tty, as in a `/bin/sh` pane, would be dropped silently, so such a launch is written to `agent-launch.sh` in the Task directory (`lead-launch.sh` in the Project directory for a Lead restart) as the exports plus a one-shot shell function named after the agent's command; the pane sources that file and `agent.start` types the bare command. A command that cannot be a POSIX function name fails at once with `launch_line_too_long`. A pane whose shell is not POSIX (`fish`, `csh`, `tcsh`, `nu`, `elvish`, `xonsh`, `pwsh`) cannot source that file and fails at once with `launch_shell_unsupported`; Worker panes need a POSIX shell for launches over the limit.
 7. Codex and OpenCode receive `Read <launch.md path> and follow it.` as an opening argument in `agent.start`; these are the existing opening-argument paths. Claude and Pi first become interactively ready, then receive the instruction through a focused-safe typed prompt because Herdr keeps an opening-argument start pending for the first turn, which prevents steering. Focus does not delay launch. After Herdr observes the first turn working, the Task becomes `working`. Relaunch uses the same per-kind delivery paths with `relaunch.md`. If recovery of an interrupted Claude or Pi start cannot type the prompt because its pane is focused, the Task remains `spawning` for a later retry; Posse raises a `spawn_waiting` Notice if that recovery remains unresolved past the launch retry window. A failed spawn with a written Brief keeps its initialized Sandbox for `posse relaunch` on the same branch.
